==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l/en|synæsthesia}} {{qualifier|chiefly British}}
* [[synesthesia]] {{qualifier|US}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|grc|en}} {{term|lang=grc|σύν||with|tr=sun}} + {{term|lang=grc|αἴσθησις||sensation|tr=aisthēsis}}, modeled after {{term|anaesthesia|lang=en}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˌsɪnɛsˈθiːʒə/}}
* {{rhymes|iːʒə}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{context|neurology|psychology|lang=en}} A [[neurological]] or [[psychological]] [[phenomenon]] whereby a particular [[sensory]] [[stimulus]] triggers a second kind of [[sensation]].
#* '''1984''', {{w|William Gibson}}, ''{{w|Neuromancer}}'',
#*: Into her darkness, a churning '''synaesthesia''', where her pain was the taste of old iron, scent of melon, wings of a moth brushing her cheek.
#* '''2002''', Sean A. Day, ''What '''synaesthesia''' is (and is not)'', Paul Mc Kevitt, Seán Ó Nualláin, Conn Mulvihill (editors), ''Language, Vision and Music: Selected Papers from the 8th International Workshop on the Cognitive Science of Natural Language Processing, Galway, 1999'', [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=OyRZAQL0x6QC&pg=PA171&dq=%22synaesthesia%22+--intitle:%22synaesthesia%22&hl=en&ei=le7UTo6mGaqPiAfN7qzwDQ&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=1&ved=0CDAQ6AEwAA#v=onepage&q=%22synaesthesia%22%20--intitle%3A%22synaesthesia%22&f=false page 171],
#*: For example, I myself have a type of '''synaesthesia''': The sounds of musical instruments will sometimes make me see colors, about a yard in front of me, each color specific and consistent with the particular instrument playing.
#* '''2009''', Graham Richards, ''Psychology: The Key Concepts'', [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=_p_DYj_UdKcC&pg=PA244&dq=%22synaesthesia%22+--intitle:%22synaesthesia%22&hl=en&ei=le7UTo6mGaqPiAfN7qzwDQ&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=6&ved=0CEoQ6AEwBQ#v=onepage&q=%22synaesthesia%22%20--intitle%3A%22synaesthesia%22&f=false page 244],
#*: '''Synaesthesia''' can occur particularly powerfully during mescalin and LSD intoxication, and is often given mystical significance.
# The association of one sensory perception with, or description of it in terms of, another, unlike, perception that is not experienced at the same time.
#* '''2007''', Boris Wiseman, ''Lévi-Strauss, Anthropology, and Aesthetics'', [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=pOJTWmu9rlMC&pg=PA112&dq=%22synaesthesia%22+--intitle:%22synaesthesia%22&hl=en&ei=B_bUTuGfDsOiiAfcxaCLDw&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=2&ved=0CDQQ6AEwATgo#v=onepage&q=%22synaesthesia%22%20--intitle%3A%22synaesthesia%22&f=false page 112],
#*: For one of the enigmatic features of '''synaesthesia''' is that, within a given cultural group, the kinds of associations made by specific subjects occur according to statistically verifiable recurring patterns. As Jakobson explains, ‘When we ask whether /i/ or /u/ is darker, testing such phonic oppositions as grave vs. acute, some of the subjects may respond that this question makes no sense to them, but hardly one will respond that /i/ is the darker of the two’ (1981:44).
# A literary or artistic device whereby one kind of sensation is described in the terms of another.
#* '''2006''', Stephen Bowkett, ''Boys and Writing'', [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=DGxa0WXX2B4C&pg=PT39&dq=%22synaesthesia%22+--intitle:%22synaesthesia%22&hl=en&ei=b_TUTu7uLcaciAfmqrlz&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=5&ved=0CEMQ6AEwBDgU#v=onepage&q=%22synaesthesia%22%20--intitle%3A%22synaesthesia%22&f=false page 38],
#*: Linking moods with colours is one example of '''synaesthesia'''.
#* '''2007''', Roger Beebe, Jason Middleton, ''Medium Cool: Music Videos from Soundies to Cellphones'', [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=8f_-1giPRFoC&pg=PA181&dq=%22synaesthesia%22+--intitle:%22synaesthesia%22&hl=en&ei=_-7VTs7JNYuXiQeLhpyGDw&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=6&ved=0CEQQ6AEwBThG#v=onepage&q=%22synaesthesia%22%20--intitle%3A%22synaesthesia%22&f=false page 181],
#*: {{...}}it may be stated that the concept of '''synaesthesia''' is instrumental for understanding music videos, since videos are based on the soundtrack′s visual associations.<sup>28</sup>

